RDMA-Tutorial example 2 (RC RDMA write) over RoCE, for rocm-ernic

Applied by ansible/playbooks/tutorial-tests.yml to
https://github.com/jcxue/RDMA-Tutorial at commit
f73736ed7a7d496f9acfc899e5d1997e791718ed. RDMA-Tutorial is licensed
under Apache-2.0, and so is this patch.

Upstream addresses the peer by InfiniBand LID. The emulated ionic NIC
is RoCE, which addresses it by GID, so this:

- Exchanges each side's IPv4-mapped GID along with its QP number, and
  connects the QP over a global route to the peer's.
- Uses the port's active MTU rather than a hardcoded 4096.
- Opens the device RDMA_TUTORIAL_DEV names, if it is set, rather than
  the first one.
- Lets TOT_NUM_OPS and NUM_WARMING_UP_OPS be set at build time: the
  defaults take tens of minutes over the emulated NIC.
- Links libibverbs, which the example calls directly, instead of
  librdmacm, which it never calls and which the guest image has no
  link-time library for.
- Includes <stdlib.h> for atoi(), which GCC 14 and later reject
  undeclared.
- Sets SO_REUSEADDR on the server's socket before binding it: every run
  uses the same port, and a rerun must not fail with EADDRINUSE while
  the last run's connection is still in TIME_WAIT.
- Numbers the messages, from 1, in the first and last 8 bytes of each,
  and waits for both ends of a message before taking it as arrived and
  clearing the slot, rather than for either end to read 'A'. The order in
  which a WRITE's bytes land isn't defined, so a reader that cleared the
  slot on the first byte could have the rest of the WRITE land after the
  clear, and count it as the next message. The receiver checks each
  message's number, so one that lands twice or never is reported, and
  the program fails at once, rather than the two sides' counts drifting
  apart until one waits forever. A message whose ends disagree gets
  100 ms to finish landing. Every message is sent from one buffer, so
  num_concurr_msgs must be 1, and msg_size a multiple of 8 and at least
  16.
- Says how many messages it had received when timeout(1) stops it, so a
  run that hangs shows how far it got.
- Has the client wait for its last WRITE to complete before it exits.
  The server waits for that WRITE, but upstream posts it unsignaled and
  exits at once, and exiting destroys the QP, which may drop a WRITE
  the NIC hasn't sent yet. The client then passed while the server
  waited out its timeout. The last WRITE is now signaled, and the
  client polls until it completes or fails.

